    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nNvim can't display Hebrew or Arabic in reading order. `rightleft` mirrors the whole window, so it only works when the line is Hebrew-only. If you mix with a Latin word / number it comes out flipped.\r\n\r\nAs `:help rileft.txt` says \"Vim currently, due to simplicity, does not offer bidi\".\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nReorder each line as it is drawn, the way #25905 mirrors one for 'rightleft'.\r\n\r\nNew window-local 'bidi', off by default, sets the direction of a paragraph: `\"\"`, `\"auto\"` (from its first strong character), `\"ltr\"`, `\"rtl\"`.\r\n\r\n- Latin words, numbers and dates inside Hebrew keep their own order. Brackets and quotes around Hebrew are drawn facing the text they enclose.\r\n- The cursor stays on the character it is on. `h` and `l`, the arrow keys, and `<` and `>` all follow the direction of the line, so `h` always moves left and `<` always shifts the text left.\r\n- The sign, number and fold columns stay where they are. Virtual text is given its own direction, so a Latin inlay hint on a Hebrew line stays readable and doesn't flip the line it annotates.\r\n- Ignored when 'termbidi' or 'rightleft' is set.\r\n\r\nThe new file is `src/nvim/bidi.c`: it resolves which direction each character runs in and what order to draw a line in. It implements a subset of [UAX #9](https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr9/) and each block cites the rule it comes from. The character classes come from the bundled utf8proc, so this adds no dependency.\r\n\r\n`test/unit/bidi_spec.lua` checks the algorithm against Unicode's conformance data, `BidiCharacterTest.txt`; `bidi_cases.py` regenerates the cases from a current copy of that file.\r\n\r\nWith 'bidi' set, `grid_line` carries cells in visual order instead of logical order. The event shape doesn't change and no client (Neovide etc) needs a code change.\r\n\r\nRelated:\r\n\r\n- https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/553\r\n\r\n",
